Rewritten: the whole teaching path, in Common Lisp and macOS's own programs. The
teach_screen tool and its six actions -- capture, draw, clear, list, status,
check -- with every argument it took (target, shapes with their kinds,
colours, step badges, captions, dashed outlines and rect fills, the lesson
title, steps and current step, the legend corner, the capture id, a TTL); the
window list through osascript and System Events, with the app, title, position
and size of each window and the refusal that names what is visible when a
target matches nothing; the screenshot through screencapture and its shrink to
a 1280-pixel JPEG through sips; the rule that a capture's frame size, bounds
and app are remembered so a draw needs no numbers from the model, that a
capture id older than the newest capture is refused, that a window which moved
is followed while one which was resized is refused, and that a mark outside
the picture it claims is refused; the shape validation and its limits; the
legend corner picked away from the marks; the marks and the step card, drawn as
a borderless always-on-top transparent overlay with the original's colours and
halo badges; the TTL, the click that dismisses the marks, the idle exit, and
the permission check with the one fix for each miss; the /teach command with
check, demo, status, clear and stop; and the manual topic the original's skill
taught from.

Left out: the Hermes-only surfaces -- the Desktop status bar chip, its palette
commands and the JSON socket IPC between the plugin and its overlay, which
became a scene file the overlay polls; the Python Tk runtime, replaced by a
Tcl/Tk script the cell writes, so Tcl/Tk must be on the Mac rather than inside
Python; the pulsing badge of the current step; and click-through. The original
reached into its own NSWindow through ctypes to make the marks ignore the
mouse, which Tcl/Tk cannot do without an Objective-C bridge, so here a click on
the marks takes them off instead of passing through. The bundled SKILL.md is
not shipped as a skill file: its guidance is the tool description and the
:teach manual topic, which ride every request and answer (help :teach).
